> 
> Is this a limitation of the LLM being provided via ACP @aweis89 ? Regular models do respect this setting.

Yes, ACP is designed to have the backend ACP provider execute the tools on it's own. This is unlike a regular model which delegates tool execution to OpenCode. Therefore OpenCode cannot enforce which tools get executed and this needs to be configured via the backend provider instead. I should probably document this as a key limitation when using an ACP backend.

Documentation clearly state `@general` as subagent, it makes sense you can't `@` a subagent, otherwise you need to make it of mode type `all` in the config:

```json
"agent": {
  "general": {
    "mode": "all"
  }
},
```

> Is this something Github changed recently? I had reasoningEffort working with GPT 5.2 and now it no longer works (no change to configuration on my end).

@jbuckmccready This is a change in OpenCode, not GitHub. 

Commit f1bb5870 (merged December 1st) broke reasoning support for GPT-5.2 and other non-Codex GPT-5 models. The change incorrectly assumed only Codex models support reasoning, so it only routes models with "codex" in the name to the API that supports `reasoningEffort`. Everything else goes to a different API that doesn't support it.

GPT-5.2 was working with reasoning before this commit. There's a [PR](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/pull/5877) to fix it.

